## Type Safety (Dart 3.8+)

- Always specify explicit types
- Never use `dynamic` or `Object?`
- Avoid implicit casts completely
- Use strict type inference
- Never use `!` without verification
- Prefer `late` over `!` operators
- Use nullable types explicitly

```dart
// Good: explicit types
final List<String> names = <String>[];
final Map<String, int> scores = <String, int>{};

// Bad: implicit dynamic
final names = [];
final scores = {};
```

## Language Features

- Use sound null safety
- Prefer `async`/`await` over callbacks
- Use `Future` for single async operations
- Use `Stream` for async sequences
- Apply sealed classes for enums
- Use records for multiple returns
- Prefer exhaustive switch statements
- Use super parameters in constructors

## Pattern Matching (Dart 3+)

- Use pattern matching in switches
- Apply destructuring with records
- Prefer exhaustive case coverage
- Use when guards for conditions
- Apply object patterns for classes

```dart
// Switch expressions with patterns
String processState(FeatureState state) => switch (state) {
  InitialState() => 'Starting...',
  LoadingState(message: final msg?) => 'Loading: $msg',
  SuccessState<String>(data: final data) => 'Success: $data',
  ErrorState(message: final error) => 'Error: $error',
  _ => 'Unknown state'
};

// Record destructuring
final (String name, int age) = getUserInfo();
```

## Extension Methods

- Create extensions for utility functions
- Avoid global utility classes
- Group related functionality together
- Use descriptive extension names
- Follow @lib/core/utils/ patterns

```dart
extension StringValidation on String {
  bool get isValidEmail => Validators.isValidEmail(this);
  bool get isNotEmpty => Validators.isNotEmpty(this);
}

// Usage
if (email.isValidEmail) { /* handle */ }
```

## Code Style

- Use `PascalCase` for classes
- Use `camelCase` for members/functions
- Use `snake_case` for files
- Keep functions under 20 lines
- Single purpose per function
- Use arrow syntax for one-liners
- Group related classes in library
- Organize libraries by feature
- Prefer `final` for all variables
- Use `const` constructors when possible

## Naming Conventions

- Avoid abbreviations in names
- Use meaningful descriptive names
- Be consistent across codebase
- Follow @lib/core/constants/app_constants.dart patterns
- Match existing service naming @lib/services/interfaces/

## Error Handling

- Use `try-catch` for exceptions
- Create custom exceptions when needed
- Never let code fail silently
- Throw exceptions early
- Handle errors at appropriate level
- Follow @lib/core/errors/exceptions.dart patterns

## Documentation

- Use `///` for doc comments
- Start with single-sentence summary
- Document all public APIs
- Explain why, not what
- Avoid redundant documentation
- Include code examples when helpful

## Best Practices

- Follow Effective Dart guidelines
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use immutable data structures
- Apply SOLID principles
- Write testable code
- Use `logging` package over `print`
- Define types explicitly everywhere
- Never use dynamic typing
- Prefer sealed classes over enums
- Use records for data transfer
- Apply extension methods over utilities
- Use super parameters in constructors

```dart
// Good: sealed class hierarchy
sealed class ApiResult<T> {
  const ApiResult();
}

final class Success<T> extends ApiResult<T> {
  const Success(this.data);
  final T data;
}

final class Failure<T> extends ApiResult<T> {
  const Failure(this.error);
  final String error;
}

// Good: super parameters
class ServerException extends AppException {
  const ServerException(super.message, [super.code]);
}
```